[Applause]  a day of political drama and violence  after iraq's powerful shiite leader  muktara al-sadr announced his withdrawal  from politics  his supporters broke into the  presidential palace in baghdad's  fortified green zone which houses the  prime minister's office  [Music]  many even taking to the palace pool  our demands are to dissolve parliament  and to hold the corrupt to account and  we say my master mukta al-sadr their  pride is under your feet and will  sacrifice for you victory is ours  caretaker prime minister mustafar  al-karmi halted cabinet sessions until  further notice  but iraq's political life was already in  limbo there's been deadlock for almost a  year since muktara al-sada's party won  the largest share of seats in elections  last october  but it wasn't enough to secure a  majority and sarah hasn't been able to  form a government of his choosing he  told his mps to resign in june and his  supporters have been protesting since  july some even briefly occupied  parliament  they want new elections without the  participation of iran-backed groups  [Applause]  this is an iraqi revolution iraq should  be returned to its people  this is not the first time sars  announced his retirement from politics  and many have dismissed it as a bluff to  gain leverage over his rivals  already several protesters have been  killed or wounded and it's feared  sardar's latest move could lead to yet  more instability  alexia brian al-jazeera

